Below is one possible fix: we inject a new `type-annotation` token into the F# grammar so that any “`: TypeName`” (or “`(x: TypeName)`”) sequences are picked up and highlighted. The root cause is that the existing Prism F# definition never recognizes the colon‐type annotation syntax, so things like `(a: MyRecord)` or `let c: double = 1.0` fall through and aren’t tokenized as types.

### 1) Bug localization
All the highlighting rules for F# live in `components/prism-fsharp.js`. You can see that after extending the base (“clike”) it only defines comments, keywords, strings, numbers and then a single `preprocessor` insert. There is no rule for “`: TypeName`”, which is why your annotations are never colored.

### 2) Patch
We add an extra `Prism.languages.insertBefore('fsharp', 'operator', …)` right after the existing preprocessor rule. This new rule will match colon‐style annotations, split out the `:` as punctuation and the identifier after it as a `type`.

